{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["And the reason of <b>“and if”</b> is as a commandment of obligation, meaning: if the time arrives that you merit to inherit the land and to build for Me an altar of stones in the chosen house, beware lest you build them of hewn stones, thinking to do so for the grandeur of the building. And the opinion of R. Abraham ibn Ezra is that the commandment concerns the altar of the covenant mentioned in the parashah of “And these are the ordinances” (Exodus 24:4), as he writes in his commentary.\n<br>And by way of truth, the verses are in sequence: <b>You have seen that from the heavens I spoke with you</b> — through My great name; <b>you shall not make</b> alongside Me <b>gods of silver and gods of gold</b>; but I permit you to make an altar for Me alone and to sacrifice upon it burnt offerings as well as peace offerings, <b>in every place where I cause My name to be mentioned</b>, for <b>I will come to you and bless you</b> — with blessings of heaven from above, blessings of the deep that couches beneath (Genesis 49:25). And “I will mention (אַזְכִיר)” is from the form “may He bless us, our remembrance (זְכָרֵנוּ)” (Psalms 115:12)." "<b>For your sword you have waved over it</b> — to forbid upon them any contact with iron, as it says, “You shall build the altar of YHWH your Elohim of whole stones” (Deuteronomy 27:6), “You shall not wave iron over them” (Deuteronomy 27:5). And here Scripture mentions iron in the language of “sword,” for every iron implement whose mouth cuts is called a חֶרֶב (sword). Thus of the dagger it says, “Ehud made him a חֶרֶב and it had two edges” (Judges 3:16); and of the razor, “Take yourself a sharp חֶרֶב” (Ezekiel 5:1); and of saws and axes with which one trims a building, “He shall break down your towers with his חֲרָבוֹת (swords/iron tools)” (Ezekiel 26:9). So too this one who hews stones — Scripture calls it a חֶרֶב.\n\nAnd the reason for the commandment, in the words of our Sages (Mekhilta here), is for the adornment of the commandment, that the one who shortens not wave over the one who lengthens. And R. Abraham ibn Ezra said that no remnant of the chipped-off stone should remain in the refuse heaps, and that the small portion is built into the altar of the Name. Or, that one should not take from it to make an altar for idolatry, for its worshippers do so and might succeed. And the Rav said in the Guide for the Perplexed (III:45) that it is a distancing, that they not come to make in them a form and the stone become an attractive cult-stone (מַשְׂכִּית), for such was the practice of idolaters.\n\nAnd I say that the reason for the commandment is because iron is a sword — it is the destroyer of the world, and therefore it is so called. Now Esau, whom HaShem hated (Malachi 1:3), is the inheritor of the sword, for it was said to him, “By your sword you shall live” (Genesis 27:40). And the sword is his power in heaven and on earth, for in Mars and in the zodiacal signs of blood the sword succeeds, and in them his might appears. Therefore it shall not be brought into the house of HaShem. And this is the reason that Scripture explained explicitly why <b>you shall not build them of hewn stone</b>: for when you wave any iron over them to make them thus, <b>you have waved over it</b> your sword — the murderous weapon that multiplies corpses — and you have profaned it.\n\nAnd for this reason there was no iron in the Tabernacle, for even its pegs, which were better than iron, he made of copper. And likewise in the eternal house (the Temple), no iron vessel was made in it except for the knives, because slaughter is not an act of divine service. And Scripture did not forbid building of hewn stone except when one waves iron over them, for it specified “when you wave your sword over it,” and it is explicitly stated “You shall not wave iron over them” (Deuteronomy 27:5). And if one comes to dress them with silver tools or with the שָׁמִיר (shamir) that our Sages mentioned (Sotah 48b), this is permissible even though they are not whole stones. And this refutes the reasoning of R. Abraham ibn Ezra. Also the reasoning of the Rav, for this very reason, is not correct.\n\nNow Solomon added to the commandment that no iron tool at all was heard in the house while it was being built (I Kings 6:7), even though it would have been permitted. For thus we learned in the Mekhilta (here): “You shall not build them of hewn stone” — with it, i.e., the altar, you do not build, but you do build in the Hekhal and the Holy of Holies. And how do I fulfill “hammers and the axe, any iron tool, were not heard in the house while it was being built”? In the house it was not heard, but outside it was heard. And the matter was this: they would quarry the stones from their mountains with iron tools and dress them there with iron, and likewise they would cut with iron the timbers and cedars that were in the house. And thus it is written (I Kings 5:31), “They transported great stones, precious stones, to found the house, hewn stones.” And when they would bring them to the house to build the walls, they would not dress them with iron nor wave it over them at all, as is the way of builders.\n\nAnd this is what it says (I Kings 6:7), “A whole stone was brought, was it built” — not that it was whole entirely, but that it was whole in that it had no blemish sufficient to engage a fingernail; rather it was smooth and even. And the meaning of מַסָּע (transported) is that it was large as when they transported it from the mountain: they did not split the rock into many stones as is the practice of builders, nor did they dress it on site, nor would they set it with hammers and the axe as is the practice of every other building. For Solomon did not want the sound of iron to be heard in any building and throughout the entire mountain of the house — all this to distance iron from it. And this accords with the opinion of Rabbi Yirmeyah in tractate Sotah (48b). But according to the opinion of Rabbi Yehudah (there), the hewn stones he placed in his own house, not in the Temple. And according to his opinion, the meaning of the verse is “They transported great stones, precious stones, to found the house, hewn stones” (I Kings 5:31) — meaning that they also transported hewn stones, and they were for his own house. And so it appears from the plain sense of Scripture, for the courtyard he made of hewn stones, as it is written (I Kings 6:36), “He built the courtyard of three courses of hewn stone.” And he was stringent only regarding the Hekhal and the Devir. And all this is the distancing of iron from the sanctuary.\n\nAnd as for the iron that David consecrated, whose weight was not tracked (I Chronicles 22:14) — it was for making tools to cut the timbers and to hew the stones."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {0 "The final clause cites Psalms 115:12; the word play involves the root זכר in 'אַזְכִיר' and 'זְכָרֵנוּ', rendered here to preserve the lexical connection.", 1 "The Mekhilta passage 'לא תבנה אתהן גזית, בו אי אתה בונה' plays on reading the feminine suffix of אתהן as referring back to מזבח (altar), which Ramban preserves as a midrashic re-punctuation. The phrase 'המקצר על המאריך' refers to the talmudic explanation (Megillah 31b) that the altar shortens human life while the Temple lengthens it, hence no iron (which shortens) over stones (which lengthen)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:12:59.654005584Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}